1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:05,150
November 2000.

2
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:07,019
A satellite looks down at a patch of

3
00:00:07,019 --> 00:00:08,400
the Persian Gulf.

4
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:10,293
There is no island here.

5
00:00:10,293 --> 00:00:11,175
Just sea.

6
00:00:11,558 --> 00:00:13,520
November 2003.

7
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:14,797
The same place.

8
00:00:14,797 --> 00:00:15,877
A palm tree,

9
00:00:15,877 --> 00:00:17,003
fully formed.

10
00:00:17,445 --> 00:00:18,977
Between these two pictures:

11
00:00:18,977 --> 00:00:19,929
three years.

12
00:00:20,208 --> 00:00:22,901
More than two decades after that first image,

13
00:00:22,901 --> 00:00:26,849
an astronaut aboard the International Space Station photographs

14
00:00:26,849 --> 00:00:27,731
this coast.

15
00:00:28,172 --> 00:00:31,086
The palm-shaped islands stand out from orbit.

16
00:00:31,690 --> 00:00:33,466
So how do you build land where there

17
00:00:33,466 --> 00:00:34,813
was only sea?

18
00:00:34,813 --> 00:00:36,671
And what happened to the plans that came

19
00:00:36,671 --> 00:00:37,298
next?

20
00:00:39,702 --> 00:00:41,223
This is Dubai,

21
00:00:41,223 --> 00:00:43,104
in the United Arab Emirates,

22
00:00:43,104 --> 00:00:44,996
on the shore of the Persian Gulf.

23
00:00:45,507 --> 00:00:47,643
A coastline seems like the one thing on

24
00:00:47,643 --> 00:00:49,745
a map that nobody gets to choose.

25
00:00:50,128 --> 00:00:52,276
Dubai put that idea to the test.

26
00:00:52,659 --> 00:00:53,611
To see how,

27
00:00:53,611 --> 00:00:55,607
we go back to the year 2000.

28
00:00:56,118 --> 00:00:57,453
Here is that year,

29
00:00:57,453 --> 00:00:59,671
seen by an instrument called ASTER,

30
00:00:59,671 --> 00:01:01,702
on NASA's Terra satellite.

31
00:01:02,086 --> 00:01:04,001
The city hugs the shore.

32
00:01:04,001 --> 00:01:06,764
Inland, there is mostly empty desert.

33
00:01:07,275 --> 00:01:09,586
Offshore, where the palm will appear,

34
00:01:09,586 --> 00:01:11,199
nothing has been built at all.

35
00:01:11,582 --> 00:01:14,682
NASA's record includes twelve images of this area

36
00:01:14,682 --> 00:01:17,364
between 2000 and 2011.

37
00:01:17,364 --> 00:01:19,314
This is our baseline.

38
00:01:19,825 --> 00:01:22,948
Dubai's ambitions went far beyond one island.

39
00:01:23,389 --> 00:01:26,617
NASA describes the city building hundreds of artificial

40
00:01:26,617 --> 00:01:28,196
islands along its coast,

41
00:01:28,196 --> 00:01:30,982
to expand its beachfront for tourism.

42
00:01:30,982 --> 00:01:33,966
Plans from the 1990s for one project,

43
00:01:33,966 --> 00:01:35,592
called The World,

44
00:01:35,592 --> 00:01:38,633
included about three hundred small islands.

45
00:01:38,633 --> 00:01:40,920
And as recently as the early 2000s,

46
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:43,916
NASA notes, little had been built here beyond

47
00:01:43,916 --> 00:01:45,576
shallow Gulf water.

48
00:01:45,576 --> 00:01:46,935
That was the blank page.

49
00:01:47,143 --> 00:01:48,897
February 2002.

50
00:01:49,849 --> 00:01:52,414
About fifteen months after the empty-sea picture,

51
00:01:52,414 --> 00:01:55,654
the first early stage of an archipelago appears.

52
00:01:55,654 --> 00:01:57,430
This is Palm Jumeirah,

53
00:01:57,430 --> 00:01:59,624
the first of Dubai's palm islands to be

54
00:01:59,624 --> 00:02:00,077
built.

55
00:02:00,588 --> 00:02:02,434
By October 2002,

56
00:02:02,434 --> 00:02:04,245
sandy fronds are visible,

57
00:02:04,245 --> 00:02:06,428
with a circular breakwater around them.

58
00:02:06,869 --> 00:02:08,622
That ring is a clue.

59
00:02:09,005 --> 00:02:11,339
This is land with its own defense against

60
00:02:11,339 --> 00:02:12,291
open water.

61
00:02:12,732 --> 00:02:15,843
NASA calls these circular storm barriers.

62
00:02:16,052 --> 00:02:17,689
So where does the land come from?

63
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:19,906
From the sea floor itself.

64
00:02:20,510 --> 00:02:22,391
Sand was dredged from the bottom of the

65
00:02:22,391 --> 00:02:25,444
Gulf and placed where the island would be,

66
00:02:25,444 --> 00:02:28,939
with rock breakwaters to protect it from erosion.

67
00:02:28,939 --> 00:02:31,609
The material for the island was already there,

68
00:02:31,609 --> 00:02:32,457
underwater.

69
00:02:32,898 --> 00:02:35,220
But loose sand is not ground you can

70
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:35,974
build on.

71
00:02:36,485 --> 00:02:40,003
NASA's description of the method includes two details.

72
00:02:40,003 --> 00:02:43,277
Ships guided by GPS placed the sand.

73
00:02:43,277 --> 00:02:46,272
And a machine called a vibroflot compacted it

74
00:02:46,272 --> 00:02:48,908
with stone, solidifying the land.

75
00:02:49,512 --> 00:02:53,830
Compare October 2002 with November 2003.

76
00:02:53,830 --> 00:02:56,570
First, sandy fronds inside their ring.

77
00:02:56,953 --> 00:02:59,147
Then, a complete palm.

78
00:02:59,530 --> 00:03:01,435
Just over a year apart.

79
00:03:01,435 --> 00:03:03,443
The pictures can't show us how the work

80
00:03:03,443 --> 00:03:04,883
went day by day,

81
00:03:04,883 --> 00:03:07,520
only where it stood on each date.

82
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:09,494
But they do show the ring already in

83
00:03:09,494 --> 00:03:11,827
place in October 2002,

84
00:03:11,827 --> 00:03:13,964
while the fronds are still sand.

85
00:03:14,335 --> 00:03:16,634
The buildings and vegetation come later,

86
00:03:16,634 --> 00:03:19,919
in the images from 2004 to 2008.

87
00:03:20,349 --> 00:03:23,611
After 2003, the images change in a different

88
00:03:23,611 --> 00:03:27,129
way. From 2004 through 2008,

89
00:03:27,129 --> 00:03:30,032
ASTER shows buildings and vegetation appearing on the

90
00:03:30,032 --> 00:03:32,690
palm. The land was no longer just a

91
00:03:32,690 --> 00:03:34,966
shape. It was becoming a place.

92
00:03:35,570 --> 00:03:37,903
Palm Jumeirah was only the first.

93
00:03:38,344 --> 00:03:40,260
By September 2006,

94
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:42,641
NASA was describing the palm islands as the

95
00:03:42,641 --> 00:03:44,986
largest artificial islands in the world,

96
00:03:44,986 --> 00:03:46,716
and every one of them was still under

97
00:03:46,716 --> 00:03:47,517
construction.

98
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:51,185
That 2006 image shows four projects in a

99
00:03:51,185 --> 00:03:53,090
row, from south to north:

100
00:03:53,473 --> 00:03:55,040
Palm Jebel Ali,

101
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:56,956
Palm Jumeirah, The World,

102
00:03:56,956 --> 00:03:58,105
and Palm Deira.

103
00:03:58,709 --> 00:04:01,902
Jebel Ali and Jumeirah already look largely complete

104
00:04:01,902 --> 00:04:03,039
as landforms.

105
00:04:03,422 --> 00:04:05,640
The World is still under construction.

106
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:08,264
Palm Deira is in its earliest stages.

107
00:04:08,589 --> 00:04:11,456
Palm Jumeirah and Palm Jebel Ali were shaped

108
00:04:11,456 --> 00:04:12,954
into palm trees.

109
00:04:12,954 --> 00:04:15,090
The third project is not a palm.

110
00:04:15,090 --> 00:04:16,820
The World is laid out as a map

111
00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:18,434
of Earth's continents.

112
00:04:18,434 --> 00:04:20,895
The plans called for about three hundred small

113
00:04:20,895 --> 00:04:23,031
islands, arranged to draw the planet in the

114
00:04:23,031 --> 00:04:23,542
sea.

115
00:04:23,925 --> 00:04:26,758
Then came 2007 and 2008,

116
00:04:26,758 --> 00:04:28,175
and the global recession.

117
00:04:28,558 --> 00:04:29,568
According to NASA,

118
00:04:29,568 --> 00:04:31,437
it delayed these projects.

119
00:04:31,437 --> 00:04:33,318
In the 2006 image,

120
00:04:33,318 --> 00:04:35,651
you can see how much was still ahead:

121
00:04:35,651 --> 00:04:37,695
Palm Deira had barely begun,

122
00:04:37,695 --> 00:04:39,982
and The World was still under construction.

123
00:04:40,307 --> 00:04:42,884
Now jump ahead to 2022.

124
00:04:42,884 --> 00:04:45,798
NASA's caption for the astronaut's photograph says only

125
00:04:45,798 --> 00:04:48,237
a handful of The World's roughly three hundred

126
00:04:48,237 --> 00:04:50,164
islands had seen development.

127
00:04:50,164 --> 00:04:52,463
That is a statement about 2022,

128
00:04:52,463 --> 00:04:54,297
and we will stay inside it.

129
00:04:54,297 --> 00:04:55,458
We have no reliable,

130
00:04:55,458 --> 00:04:57,304
dated source for what has happened since,

131
00:04:57,304 --> 00:04:58,557
so we will not guess.

132
00:04:59,672 --> 00:05:02,098
Two more projects followed the same pattern.

133
00:05:02,482 --> 00:05:05,895
Palm Deira, in its earliest stages in 2006,

134
00:05:05,895 --> 00:05:08,948
was scaled back and renamed the Deira Islands.

135
00:05:08,948 --> 00:05:10,179
And The Universe,

136
00:05:10,179 --> 00:05:11,665
another planned archipelago,

137
00:05:11,665 --> 00:05:13,023
was put on hold.

138
00:05:13,023 --> 00:05:14,579
Delayed, scaled back,

139
00:05:14,579 --> 00:05:17,644
on hold: those words describe plans,

140
00:05:17,644 --> 00:05:19,258
not the condition of the land.

141
00:05:19,536 --> 00:05:23,252
NASA describes one basic method for Dubai's islands:

142
00:05:23,252 --> 00:05:26,084
dredged sand, protected by rock.

143
00:05:26,084 --> 00:05:27,582
One design is a palm.

144
00:05:27,582 --> 00:05:29,335
The other is a map of Earth.

145
00:05:29,335 --> 00:05:32,505
By 2011, vegetation covered most of Palm Jumeirah's

146
00:05:32,505 --> 00:05:35,221
fronds, and many buildings stood on its trunk.

147
00:05:35,663 --> 00:05:38,519
In 2022, only a handful of The World's

148
00:05:38,519 --> 00:05:40,144
islands had seen development.

149
00:05:40,748 --> 00:05:41,781
Similar method.

150
00:05:42,164 --> 00:05:43,499
Very different outcomes.

151
00:05:44,010 --> 00:05:46,355
What keeps new land from washing away?

152
00:05:46,796 --> 00:05:48,166
In NASA's account,

153
00:05:48,166 --> 00:05:50,999
rock breakwaters protect the islands from erosion.

154
00:05:51,382 --> 00:05:54,517
Around Palm Jumeirah and Palm Jebel Ali,

155
00:05:54,517 --> 00:05:56,874
they form a circular storm barrier:

156
00:05:56,874 --> 00:05:58,522
stone on the outside,

157
00:05:58,522 --> 00:06:00,148
sand on the inside.

158
00:06:00,589 --> 00:06:02,226
Now widen the view.

159
00:06:02,226 --> 00:06:05,825
In 2000, the land inland was mostly empty

160
00:06:05,825 --> 00:06:08,240
desert. By 2011,

161
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:10,202
NASA reports, roads,

162
00:06:10,202 --> 00:06:13,534
buildings and irrigated land covered almost all of

163
00:06:13,534 --> 00:06:14,347
this area.

164
00:06:14,718 --> 00:06:16,158
The coast was changing,

165
00:06:16,158 --> 00:06:17,981
and so was everything behind it.

166
00:06:18,410 --> 00:06:19,722
A note on method.

167
00:06:20,094 --> 00:06:22,253
Every image here carries its source,

168
00:06:22,253 --> 00:06:24,447
its instrument and its year.

169
00:06:24,958 --> 00:06:26,677
Most come from ASTER,

170
00:06:26,677 --> 00:06:28,430
an instrument on a satellite.

171
00:06:28,871 --> 00:06:31,251
One is a photograph taken by an astronaut

172
00:06:31,251 --> 00:06:32,702
with a Nikon camera.

173
00:06:33,085 --> 00:06:36,081
They are separate pictures from separate days,

174
00:06:36,081 --> 00:06:37,346
not moving footage,

175
00:06:37,346 --> 00:06:39,993
and we never overlay one on another.

176
00:06:40,678 --> 00:06:41,793
For one image,

177
00:06:41,793 --> 00:06:44,800
NASA's text and caption disagree on the month,

178
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:46,367
so we give only the year.

179
00:06:46,681 --> 00:06:48,283
Lay out the whole record.

180
00:06:48,283 --> 00:06:50,349
Twelve ASTER images,

181
00:06:50,349 --> 00:06:53,704
from November 2000 to 2011.

182
00:06:53,704 --> 00:06:54,726
Empty sea.

183
00:06:54,726 --> 00:06:56,328
The first early stage.

184
00:06:56,328 --> 00:06:58,720
Sandy fronds inside a ring.

185
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:00,229
A finished palm.

186
00:07:00,229 --> 00:07:02,598
Years of buildings and vegetation.

187
00:07:02,598 --> 00:07:04,548
Each one is a dated fact.

188
00:07:04,548 --> 00:07:07,033
Together, they are a timeline you can check.

189
00:07:07,520 --> 00:07:10,841
NASA says both palm-shaped islands stand out in

190
00:07:10,841 --> 00:07:13,581
the 2022 astronaut photograph.

191
00:07:13,581 --> 00:07:16,634
Both are ringed by circular storm barriers.

192
00:07:17,017 --> 00:07:19,026
But according to NASA's caption,

193
00:07:19,026 --> 00:07:22,543
Palm Jebel Ali remains mostly undeveloped,

194
00:07:22,543 --> 00:07:25,075
though it already looked largely complete as a

195
00:07:25,075 --> 00:07:27,315
landform in 2006.

196
00:07:27,745 --> 00:07:30,659
A finished shape is not a finished city.

197
00:07:30,659 --> 00:07:32,772
So this is not a story of simple

198
00:07:32,772 --> 00:07:34,525
success or failure.

199
00:07:34,525 --> 00:07:36,592
It is a record of what was built,

200
00:07:36,592 --> 00:07:37,880
what was delayed,

201
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:39,633
and what was only planned.

202
00:07:40,051 --> 00:07:42,721
A map of this coast drawn in 2000

203
00:07:42,721 --> 00:07:44,742
would show open water here.

204
00:07:45,171 --> 00:07:46,692
By November 2003,

205
00:07:46,692 --> 00:07:48,224
that map would be wrong.

206
00:07:48,805 --> 00:07:51,591
The method NASA describes is simple to state:

207
00:07:51,591 --> 00:07:53,588
take sand from the sea floor,

208
00:07:53,588 --> 00:07:54,680
ring it with rock,

209
00:07:54,680 --> 00:07:57,257
and a coastline stops being a fixed line.

210
00:07:57,675 --> 00:07:59,672
It gave Dubai a palm.

211
00:07:59,672 --> 00:08:01,436
It also gave a plan for a map

212
00:08:01,436 --> 00:08:02,063
of Earth.

213
00:08:02,296 --> 00:08:04,792
November 2000: open sea.

214
00:08:05,570 --> 00:08:08,402
November 2003: a palm,

215
00:08:08,402 --> 00:08:09,598
inside its ring.

216
00:08:10,016 --> 00:08:13,244
In 2022, an astronaut photographs the palm islands

217
00:08:13,244 --> 00:08:14,091
from orbit.

218
00:08:14,091 --> 00:08:16,773
And NASA's caption notes that other projects were

219
00:08:16,773 --> 00:08:18,515
delayed, scaled back,

220
00:08:18,515 --> 00:08:20,001
or put on hold.

221
00:08:20,001 --> 00:08:21,916
A coastline is not only where the land

222
00:08:21,916 --> 00:08:24,807
ends. Sometimes, it is a decision.

223
00:08:24,807 --> 00:08:25,608
And from orbit,

224
00:08:25,608 --> 00:08:26,804
you can check the dates.

225
00:08:27,280 --> 00:08:30,032
Imagery: NASA Earth Observatory.

226
00:08:30,032 --> 00:08:31,773
ASTER on the Terra satellite.

227
00:08:32,168 --> 00:08:37,811
Astronaut photograph ISS067-E-3785,

228
00:08:37,811 --> 00:08:40,400
ISS Crew Earth Observations.

